  "texts": {
    "headline": "Of 3,044 AI governance statements analyzed, 503 (16.5%) explicitly mention children -- but only 30 (1.0%) pair that mention with enforcement mechanisms.",
    "keyFindings": [
      "16.5% of statements explicitly name children, minors, or youth",
      "Maximum Children Governance Index (CGI) is 64.5/100 -- no statement comprehensively addresses all 20 dimensions",
      "The mention-to-participation ratio is 503:333 -- for every statement that mentions children, only 333 include children's voice in governance",
      "Only 9 statements (0.3%) address dark patterns and addictive design targeting children",
      "The top-scoring statements are predominantly from civil society and advocacy organizations, not binding regulation",
      "Protection dimensions dominate; Participation dimensions are 36x less covered",
      "Education AI is addressed by 92 statements despite massive post-COVID expansion",
      "Only 165 statements address AI-generated CSAM, the fastest-growing threat to children"
    ],
    "methodology": "20 dimensions scored 0-100 using keyword + context heuristic pipeline designed by 12-member expert council. Scoring formula: strong*15 + medium*5 - FP*10 + title_bonus(20), strong-floor at 50. Composite CGI is expert-weighted average.",
    "council": "12 experts: statistician, NLP scientist, children's rights legal scholar, child development psychologist, digital safety advocate, AI governance scholar, civil society representative, EdTech specialist, data privacy lawyer, indigenous youth advocate, industry safety officer, intergenerational ethics philosopher."
  }
